     "transcript": [
      {
       "start": 3.4,
       "end": 8.422,
       "text": "Mark each node +1 or −1 for its side.",
       "sceneId": "a01-cutform"
      },
      {
       "start": 8.422,
       "end": 14.156,
       "text": "A same-side edge contributes 0; a crossing edge's squared difference is 4.",
       "sceneId": "a01-cutform"
      },
      {
       "start": 14.156,
       "end": 20.733,
       "text": "So xᵀLx is exactly 4 times the cut: 12 for DE | ABC.",
       "sceneId": "a01-cutform"
      },
      {
       "start": 21.133,
       "end": 29.0,
       "text": "Allow only ±1 and you must try every split. Loosen to real numbers, and it becomes an eigenvector problem.",
       "sceneId": "a02-relax"
      },
      {
       "start": 29.0,
       "end": 37.133,
       "text": "At the same unit length: the split vector gives 2.50, the Fiedler vector only 1.77.",
       "sceneId": "a02-relax"
      },
      {
       "start": 37.133,
       "end": 44.0,
       "text": "λ₂ is the relaxed minimum; the price is an answer that is no longer ±1 and must be rounded.",
       "sceneId": "a02-relax"
      },
      {
       "start": 44.4,
       "end": 49.467,
       "text": "The simplest rounding: cut by sign, trying only the threshold 0.",
       "sceneId": "a03-round"
      },
      {
       "start": 49.467,
       "end": 56.044,
       "text": "It ignores balance: on a star with a heavy leaf, one side can hold a single node.",
       "sceneId": "a03-round"
      },
      {
       "start": 56.044,
       "end": 61.978,
       "text": "This course tries all n − 1 thresholds and takes the least inside the window.",
       "sceneId": "a03-round"
      },
      {
       "start": 62.378,
       "end": 66.733,
       "text": "Why a balance window? Compare three constraints.",
       "sceneId": "a04-window"
      },
      {
       "start": 66.733,
       "end": 72.2,
       "text": "Isolating one node cuts at least 6; with no limit, the least is 3.",
       "sceneId": "a04-window"
      },
      {
       "start": 72.2,
       "end": 75.667,
       "text": "Inside the 40/60 window it is also 3.",
       "sceneId": "a04-window"
      },
      {
       "start": 75.667,
       "end": 82.244,
       "text": "“No constraint always cuts off one node” is wrong; a group can come out too small.",
       "sceneId": "a04-window"
      },
      {
       "start": 82.644,
       "end": 87.222,
       "text": "The four-node ring's eigenvalues: 0, 2, 2, 4.",
       "sceneId": "a05-degenerate"
      },
      {
       "start": 87.222,
       "end": 93.356,
       "text": "λ₂ = λ₃: the second eigenvector is not unique; any direction is valid.",
       "sceneId": "a05-degenerate"
      },
      {
       "start": 93.356,
       "end": 100.378,
       "text": "G01's gap is 6.68, a stable order; the smaller the gap, the more fragile.",
       "sceneId": "a05-degenerate"
      },
      {
       "start": 100.778,
       "end": 104.311,
       "text": "Is the vector genuine? Read the residual.",
       "sceneId": "a06-numerics"
      },
      {
       "start": 104.311,
       "end": 111.333,
       "text": "The original's three stored decimals give 0.0044; the live solve gives 10⁻¹⁵.",
       "sceneId": "a06-numerics"
      },
      {
       "start": 111.333,
       "end": 117.6,
       "text": "0.0044 is rounding: fine for display, but never recomputed when the graph changes.",
       "sceneId": "a06-numerics"
      },
      {
       "start": 117.6,
       "end": 125.067,
       "text": "Large circuits use sparse iterative methods like Lanczos, for only the smallest eigenpairs.",
       "sceneId": "a06-numerics"
      },
      {
       "start": 125.467,
       "end": 127.533,
       "text": "Six things to keep.",
       "sceneId": "a07-summary"
      },
      {
       "start": 140.533,
       "end": 145.333,
       "text": "Next: improve the cut by swapping nodes across it.",
       "sceneId": "a07-summary"
      }
     ],
